# builtin_name_not_hijacked.tkr — the project-regressor for the symbol-prefix ruling (owner,
# 2026-07-27): what is not builtin is named `teko_*`, what is builtin stays `tk_*`, and the two
# families are disjoint BY CONSTRUCTION.
#
# The defect this pins was measured, not imagined. `pub fn arena_push(control: u64)` type-checked
# as a user function, with the user's arity — and then BOTH backends emitted `tk_arena_push()`,
# the runtime's own checkpoint primitive, because they keyed their builtin tables by the callee's
# LAST SEGMENT. The user body never ran. It compiled, it linked, it executed the wrong function,
# and nothing said a word.
#
# The exit code reads back arithmetic only the user bodies can produce: `arena_push(3)` = 10 and
# `one_byte(31)` = 32, so 42. The runtime twins take no argument (`tk_arena_push`) and return a
# `str` (`tk_one_byte`) — neither can yield those numbers, so a relapse moves the exit code
# instead of passing quietly.
#
# The same project also calls the libc `getpid` through an `extern fn`. An extern exists to name
# the LITERAL C symbol; if the prefix scheme ever reached it, the object file would carry an
# undefined reference and this scenario would fail at the link, not at the assertion.

Feature: builtin_name_not_hijacked — a user function is never emitted as the runtime's twin

  Scenario: user functions whose bare names are builtins run their own bodies, and an extern still links verbatim
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "42"
